.TH CCOL_SELECTABLE_FROM_CIRCQ 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_selectable_from_circq \- build a ccol_selectable from a ccol_circular_queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_selectable_from_circq(q, dir);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro builds a ccol_selectable value, a tagged union whose type
here is ccol_selectable_circq, that wraps the ccol_circular_queue
pointer that you give, with the direction that you give.

  ccol_select_read; wait for at least one message to arrive (read-wait)
  ccol_select_write; wait until the queue has room for a send (write-wait)

This macro expands to a compound literal, so it costs nothing at run
time beyond the load of the pointer.

ccol_select() only reports readiness for either direction; it never
consumes or reserves anything itself, so the caller must then make the
call itself: ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() when the read direction wins,
and ccol_circq_try_send_zc() when the write direction wins. Because
ccol_select() claims nothing and reserves nothing, there is a
time-of-check to time-of-use gap, the same as the one in POSIX
select(2).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
q    ccol_circular_queue pointer to wrap. A NULL value makes a
     selectable that ccol_select() rejects with ccol_invalid_args.
dir  ccol_select_read or ccol_select_write.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_selectable value (not a pointer).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_circular_queue *q0 = ccol_circular_queue_create(8, NULL);
ccol_circular_queue *q1 = ccol_circular_queue_create(8, NULL);

size_t idx;
/* Read-wait on two queues: */
ccol_select_va(&idx,
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q1, ccol_select_read));
/* on ccol_success, claim the message with ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() on
   whichever of q0 and q1 idx points at */

/* Mixed: wait until q0 has room to send or q1 has a message: */
ccol_select_va(&idx,
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_write),
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q1, ccol_select_read));
.fi
